 "standard_text": "Using letters to stand for numbers, writing algebraic expressions, evaluating expressions, like terms and simplifying expressions (summary of NCERT Ganita Prakash Class 7)",
 "meta_desc": "Class 7 Expressions Using Letter-Numbers worksheet (Ganita Prakash Part 1, Chapter 4): writing algebraic expressions, notation, substitution, like terms and simplifying. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "A letter-number stands for any number",
 "key_sub": "3a means 3 × a; a² means a × a.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p>A <b>letter-number</b> such as n can stand for any number. 3n means 3 × n; n² means n × n; n + 5 means 5 more than n.</p><p>To find the <b>value</b> of an expression, replace each letter by its number: if n = 4, then 3n + 5 = 17.</p><p><b>Like terms</b> have the same letter part and can be added: 4a + 7a = 11a. 4a + 7b cannot be simplified.</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Simplify 5x + 3y − 2x + y.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>5x − 2x = 3x</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>3y + y = 4y</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 3x + 4y</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Find 4p − 7 when p = 5.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>4 × 5 − 7</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 13</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Reading 3a as 3 + a.</li><li>Writing a² as 2a.</li><li>Adding unlike terms: 2a + 3b = 5ab is wrong.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "Why do we use letters in maths?",
   "a": "A letter stands for a number we do not know yet or for any number, so one expression can describe many cases."
  },
  {
   "q": "What are like terms?",
   "a": "Terms with exactly the same letters and powers, such as 3x and −5x."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do you evaluate an expression?",
   "a": "Replace each letter by its value and follow the order of operations."
  }
 ],
